00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:28,319
Statistical distributions are very

229
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,240
important especially for understanding

230
00:08:28,319 --> 00:08:32,399
noise and random variables over

231
00:08:30,240 --> 00:08:35,200
different intervals. The simplest case

232
00:08:32,399 --> 00:08:37,360
is the uniform distribution.

233
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:40,000
With a uniform distribution, every point

234
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:42,159
in the interval like 0 to 1 or 0 to 5

235
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,919
has the same chance of being picked.

236
00:08:42,159 --> 00:08:45,920
Every number in that range is equally

237
00:08:43,919 --> 00:08:47,920
likely. That's one simple kind of

238
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:50,000
distribution.

239
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:52,560
Next, we have the Gaussian distribution

240
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,399
and after that the pluson distribution.

241
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,399
The Gaussian distribution, also called

242
00:08:54,399 --> 00:08:58,720
the normal distribution, is your classic

243
00:08:56,399 --> 00:09:00,800
bell-shaped curve. You usually describe

244
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:03,600
it with a mean called mu and a standard

245
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:05,360
deviation sigma. If mu is zero and sigma

246
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:07,279
is one, that's the standard normal

247
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:09,440
distribution.

248
00:09:07,279 --> 00:09:11,519
Mathematically, you see a scaling factor

249
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:14,000
and an exponential factor involving mu

250
00:09:11,519 --> 00:09:15,920
and sigma. This type of distribution is

251
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,959
used everywhere in engineering, noise

252
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:21,040
measurement, estimation, and more.

253
00:09:18,959 --> 00:09:23,120
If you have lots of independent random

254
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:25,200
variables, each contributing a bit,

255
00:09:23,120 --> 00:09:27,360
maybe some are uniform, maybe some are

256
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:28,560
not, when you put them all together, the

257
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:30,800
outcome will tend to look like a

258
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:32,480
Gaussian curve.

259
00:09:30,800 --> 00:09:34,720
This is a fundamental result from

260
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:36,959
statistics, and it's why you see the

261
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:39,680
bell-shaped curve over and over in

262
00:09:36,959 --> 00:09:41,120
reality. Lots of independent factors add

263
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:42,880
up to give something that looks

264
00:09:41,120 --> 00:09:45,040
Gaussian.

265
00:09:42,880 --> 00:09:46,959
The pson distribution is related to the

266
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:49,600
Gaussian but looks very different

267
00:09:46,959 --> 00:09:51,680
especially with small means. The pan

268
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:54,720
describes random variables that give you

269
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:57,120
counts like 1 2 3 and so on with

270
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:58,720
different probabilities for each count.

271
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:00,720
It's controlled by the mean which we

272
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:02,399
call lambda.

273
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:05,120
If that mean is small, you get a very

274
00:10:02,399 --> 00:10:07,440
asymmetric distribution. But as lambda

275
00:10:05,120 --> 00:10:09,279
grows larger, the curve starts to look

276
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:11,519
more and more like a Gaussian.

277
00:10:09,279 --> 00:10:13,680
Eventually, if lambda is really big, the

278
00:10:11,519 --> 00:10:16,880
pson distribution and the Gaussian

279
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:18,959
distribution look nearly the same. For

280
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:21,600
the Gaussian, you have mean and variance

281
00:10:18,959 --> 00:10:23,920
