00:31:38,799 --> 00:31:43,840
noise is not normal anymore.

838
00:31:41,679 --> 00:31:46,159
It's not the normal noise you see in CT

839
00:31:43,840 --> 00:31:47,679
reconstruction. The underlying source

840
00:31:46,159 --> 00:31:49,919
distribution can be modeled

841
00:31:47,679 --> 00:31:51,919
statistically. The image represents the

842
00:31:49,919 --> 00:31:54,559
source distribution broken down into

843
00:31:51,919 --> 00:31:56,799
small pixels where each pixel acts like

844
00:31:54,559 --> 00:31:58,240
a small light bulb.

845
00:31:56,799 --> 00:32:00,880
So far we've talked about image

846
00:31:58,240 --> 00:32:02,799
reconstruction for both spec and PET.

847
00:32:00,880 --> 00:32:04,480
This assumes your data model is good

848
00:32:02,799 --> 00:32:07,440
enough, meaning you have a large number

849
00:32:04,480 --> 00:32:09,279
of gamma photons captured.

850
00:32:07,440 --> 00:32:11,519
In that situation, the measurements

851
00:32:09,279 --> 00:32:14,720
reflect the actual statistical mean. So

852
00:32:11,519 --> 00:32:16,720
you get a formula, the data model.

853
00:32:14,720 --> 00:32:18,960
The data model is tricky for single

854
00:32:16,720 --> 00:32:20,720
photon emission and different for paired

855
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:22,960
photon emission because the waiting

856
00:32:20,720 --> 00:32:25,120
factor changes.

857
00:32:22,960 --> 00:32:27,039
For single photon emission, the waiting

858
00:32:25,120 --> 00:32:29,440
factor fundamentally depends on the

859
00:32:27,039 --> 00:32:31,279
location of the active source element.

860
00:32:29,440 --> 00:32:33,200
The waiting factor always depends on

861
00:32:31,279 --> 00:32:34,880
location.

862
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:37,360
I explained that this location

863
00:32:34,880 --> 00:32:39,039
dependence cannot be easily removed.

864
00:32:37,360 --> 00:32:40,799
When you try to multiply paired

865
00:32:39,039 --> 00:32:42,480
measurements together, cross terms

866
00:32:40,799 --> 00:32:44,960
appear and you can't easily get rid of

867
00:32:42,480 --> 00:32:46,720
the spatial dependence.

868
00:32:44,960 --> 00:32:48,480
But for PET photon emission, you're

869
00:32:46,720 --> 00:32:50,559
actually able to account for the single

870
00:32:48,480 --> 00:32:52,960
source distribution with a probability

871
00:32:50,559 --> 00:32:55,200
factor in the formula.

872
00:32:52,960 --> 00:32:56,799
If you have multiple radioactive source

873
00:32:55,200 --> 00:32:58,720
points along the same line, you just

874
00:32:56,799 --> 00:33:01,200
form the line integral by adding them

875
00:32:58,720 --> 00:33:03,200
up. The attenuation factor for any

876
00:33:01,200 --> 00:33:04,960
radioactive element along that line is

877
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:07,200
the same.

878
00:33:04,960 --> 00:33:08,799
If you know the attenuation coefficient

879
00:33:07,200 --> 00:33:10,960
called mu, you can determine the

880
00:33:08,799 --> 00:33:13,519
attenuation factor and obtain a line

881
00:33:10,960 --> 00:33:16,240
integral. This makes it possible to use

882
00:33:13,519 --> 00:33:18,080
filtered back projection just as in CT

883
00:33:16,240 --> 00:33:20,240
which is an important feature for image

884
00:33:18,080 --> 00:33:22,159
reconstruction.

885
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:24,000
It is important to review the slides

886
00:33:22,159 --> 00:33:26,000
carefully to follow the argument and

887
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:28,399
understand the data model for spec and

888
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:30,320
PET. Once that model is clear, filtered

889
00:33:28,399 --> 00:33:32,640
back projection can also be applied

890
00:33:30,320 --> 00:33:35,440
particularly in PET.

891
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:37,760
Nuclear tomography follows CT because CT

892
00:33:35,440 --> 00:33:40,000
provides the necessary foundation.

893
00:33:37,760 --> 00:33:41,919
Inspect the attenuation factor depends

894
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:44,159
on position. But if mu is known, for

895
00:33:41,919 --> 00:33:45,600
example, from a transmission scan, it

896
00:33:44,159 --> 00:33:48,559
can still be converted into the

897
00:33:45,600 --> 00:33:50,159
attenuation rate for the data.

898
00:33:48,559 --> 00:33:52,240
You can turn each measurement into

899
00:33:50,159 --> 00:33:54,720
something that forms a line integral, a

900
00:33:52,240 --> 00:33:56,960
sum. Then you'll have a system of linear

901
00:33:54,720 --> 00:34:00,320
equations, which is just like CT, a

902
00:33:56,960 --> 00:34:02,080
system of linear equations to solve.

903
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:04,880
You can use a real high performance

904
00:34:02,080 --> 00:34:07,039
computer for this. Linear algebra gives

905
00:34:04,880 --> 00:34:08,960
you the tools to measure and solve these

906
00:34:07,039 --> 00:34:12,320
equations. And we know how to do that

907
00:34:08,960 --> 00:34:13,679
both numerically and analytically.

908
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:15,679
But what happens if you don't have

909
00:34:13,679 --> 00:34:18,079
enough flux?

910
00:34:15,679 --> 00:34:20,159
That's a big problem. I mentioned it the

911
00:34:18,079 --> 00:34:22,480
other day. X-ray flux is high, but for

912
00:34:20,159 --> 00:34:23,520
nuclear imaging, it's much lower. That's

913
00:34:22,480 --> 00:34:25,919
because you're introducing

914
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:28,000
radioisotopes.

915
00:34:25,919 --> 00:34:29,760
The number of emitted gammaray photons

916
00:34:28,000 --> 00:34:31,839
isn't ideal because you don't want to

917
00:34:29,760 --> 00:34:34,079
introduce too much radioactive material

918
00:34:31,839 --> 00:34:36,480
into the human body. You need to use the

919
00:34:34,079 --> 00:34:38,480
minimum amount for safety. On the other

920
00:34:36,480 --> 00:34:40,960
hand, radioisotopes are incredibly

921
00:34:38,480 --> 00:34:42,879
sensitive. Any signal you detect comes

922
00:34:40,960 --> 00:34:46,119
from the radioisotopes. So you get very

923
00:34:42,879 --> 00:34:46,119
high sensitivity.

924
00:34:47,839 --> 00:34:51,359
We begin with the idea that each gamma

925
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:53,679
ray measurement is modeled

926
00:34:51,359 --> 00:34:56,079
independently. The underlying framework

927
00:34:53,679 --> 00:34:58,880
is a plasma model. Consider an image

928
00:34:56,079 --> 00:35:00,960
made up of pixels. Each pixel along with

929
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:02,880
its neighbors contributes independently.

930
00:35:00,960 --> 00:35:06,160
Meaning the measurement at one pixel

931
00:35:02,880 --> 00:35:08,000
does not directly depend on another.

932
00:35:06,160 --> 00:35:10,240
To capture this mathematically, we

933
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:12,720
partition time into small intervals. For

934
00:35:10,240 --> 00:35:14,400
example, 10 milliseconds each. One

935
00:35:12,720 --> 00:35:17,599
interval, the next interval, and another

936
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:19,680
further away all behave independently.

937
00:35:17,599 --> 00:35:22,079
This independence is crucial because it

938
00:35:19,680 --> 00:35:24,640
allows us to express the probability of

939
00:35:22,079 --> 00:35:27,599
the entire measurement as the product of

940
00:35:24,640 --> 00:35:29,280
many small probabilities. Each detector,

941
00:35:27,599 --> 00:35:30,960
each pixel, each time interval

942
00:35:29,280 --> 00:35:32,640
contributes its own probability and

943
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:34,960
multiplying them together gives the

944
00:35:32,640 --> 00:35:36,400
overall likelihood.

945
00:35:34,960 --> 00:35:39,119
This setup naturally leads to

946
00:35:36,400 --> 00:35:40,960
probabilistic modeling. Baze rule, which

947
00:35:39,119 --> 00:35:43,520
many of us first learned in high school,

948
00:35:40,960 --> 00:35:45,280
becomes central here. Imagine a patient

949
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:47,920
comes in and we perform data acquisition

950
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:49,440
using a camera or a PET scanner. The raw

951
00:35:47,920 --> 00:35:52,320
measurements are then organized into

952
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:54,240
centagrams in 2D or 3D.

953
00:35:52,320 --> 00:35:55,920
To understand error and uncertainty, we

954
00:35:54,240 --> 00:35:57,760
rely on the power of mathematical

955
00:35:55,920 --> 00:35:59,599
notation which is both compact and

956
00:35:57,760 --> 00:36:01,280
expressive.

957
00:35:59,599 --> 00:36:03,280
In fact, the measured counts can be

958
00:36:01,280 --> 00:36:06,000
modeled as random variables following a

959
00:36:03,280 --> 00:36:08,320
pson distribution. The problem can then

960
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:10,240
be phrased as given the measured data,

961
00:36:08,320 --> 00:36:13,200
what is the most likely underlying image

962
00:36:10,240 --> 00:36:14,960
denoted by capital lambda.

963
00:36:13,200 --> 00:36:17,520
This probability depends on several

964
00:36:14,960 --> 00:36:19,280
factors. First, assume the true image is

965
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:21,920
known along with the source distribution

966
00:36:19,280 --> 00:36:23,680
and any background activity. If we also

967
00:36:21,920 --> 00:36:25,119
know the imaging geometry, then we can

968
00:36:23,680 --> 00:36:26,880
predict what each detector should

969
00:36:25,119 --> 00:36:28,960
record.

970
00:36:26,880 --> 00:36:31,440
For example, a detector may be expected

971
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:34,320
to receive around 3,000 gamma photons

972
00:36:31,440 --> 00:36:36,320
during a 5-minut scan. The difficulty is

973
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:39,200
that in reality the image is not known.

974
00:36:36,320 --> 00:36:41,200
We only have the measured data.

975
00:36:39,200 --> 00:36:43,599
That's why we must also consider prior

976
00:36:41,200 --> 00:36:45,520
knowledge about the image distribution.

977
00:36:43,599 --> 00:36:47,440
For younger patients without cancer, we

978
00:36:45,520 --> 00:36:49,760
may expect little to no radioactive

979
00:36:47,440 --> 00:36:51,280
uptake in major organs. For older

980
00:36:49,760 --> 00:36:53,280
patients, there may be a higher

981
00:36:51,280 --> 00:36:55,599
probability of uptake, for instance, in

982
00:36:53,280 --> 00:36:57,520
the colon if there is a tumor. These

983
00:36:55,599 --> 00:36:59,440
expectations form a prior distribution

984
00:36:57,520 --> 00:37:01,359
on the image.

985
00:36:59,440 --> 00:37:03,440
However, in many cases, especially when

986
00:37:01,359 --> 00:37:06,400
data is scarce, we may not want to

987
00:37:03,440 --> 00:37:07,920
assume anything specific.

988
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:09,760
In that case, we take the prior

989
00:37:07,920 --> 00:37:12,160
distribution to be uniform, meaning

990
00:37:09,760 --> 00:37:14,320
every image is equally likely. This

991
00:37:12,160 --> 00:37:16,480
constant prior does not affect the

992
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:18,960
statistical estimation since it cancels

993
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:21,280
out in maximization.

994
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:24,560
From here, two key concepts arise.

995
00:37:21,280 --> 00:37:27,839
Maximum a posteriori map estimation and

996
00:37:24,560 --> 00:37:29,920
maximum likelihood ML estimation. Map

997
00:37:27,839 --> 00:37:32,480
seeks the image that maximizes the

998
00:37:29,920 --> 00:37:34,240
posterior probability combining both the

999
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:36,160
likelihood of the measured data given

1000
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:38,480
the image and the prior probability of

1001
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:40,720
the image itself.

1002
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:42,640
If we assume the prior is uniform, map

1003
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:45,359
reduces to maximum likelihood

1004
00:37:42,640 --> 00:37:47,200
estimation. In ML, we search for the

1005
00:37:45,359 --> 00:37:48,960
image that makes the measured data most

1006
00:37:47,200 --> 00:37:50,880
probable.

1007
00:37:48,960 --> 00:37:52,400
The process is iterative. We try

1008
00:37:50,880 --> 00:37:53,680
candidate images, compute the

1009
00:37:52,400 --> 00:37:55,760
probability that they would have

1010
00:37:53,680 --> 00:37:57,520
produced the measured data and adjust

1011
00:37:55,760 --> 00:37:59,520
until we find one with the highest

1012
00:37:57,520 --> 00:38:01,520
probability.

1013
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:04,079
That image is then reported as our

1014
00:38:01,520 --> 00:38:06,079
reconstruction. In summary, if the prior

1015
00:38:04,079 --> 00:38:08,800
distribution is non-informative, map and

1016
00:38:06,079 --> 00:38:10,560
ML give the same result. Otherwise, map

1017
00:38:08,800 --> 00:38:13,800
incorporates prior knowledge to guide

1018
00:38:10,560 --> 00:38:13,800
the estimation.

1019
00:38:15,359 --> 00:38:19,119
The IMIL approach is often used because

1020
00:38:17,359 --> 00:38:21,520
it naturally incorporates statistical

1021
00:38:19,119 --> 00:38:23,280
knowledge. To explain this more simply,

1022
00:38:21,520 --> 00:38:25,520
let us recall the idea of CT

1023
00:38:23,280 --> 00:38:27,359
reconstruction.

1024
00:38:25,520 --> 00:38:29,520
Imagine you have multiple views of an

1025
00:38:27,359 --> 00:38:31,760
object. One view looks like this.

1026
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:35,119
Another view looks different. Now you

1027
00:38:31,760 --> 00:38:37,599
try to reconstruct the underlying image.

1028
00:38:35,119 --> 00:38:39,440
Suppose the underlying image is a star.

1029
00:38:37,599 --> 00:38:41,680
When you trace the star vertically, the

1030
00:38:39,440 --> 00:38:43,599
profile does not look right. When you

1031
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:45,760
trace it horizontally, again, it does

1032
00:38:43,599 --> 00:38:48,720
not match. Then you try using a

1033
00:38:45,760 --> 00:38:50,880
different model, say an ellipse.

1034
00:38:48,720 --> 00:38:52,800
If you trace the ellipse horizontally,

1035
00:38:50,880 --> 00:38:54,960
you obtain a profile that matches the

1036
00:38:52,800 --> 00:38:56,800
measured data more closely. This

1037
00:38:54,960 --> 00:38:59,359
suggests that the elliptical model fits

1038
00:38:56,800 --> 00:39:02,400
best. In this case, you would report the

1039
00:38:59,359 --> 00:39:04,160
ellipse as the reconstructed image.

1040
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:06,560
This is essentially what maximum

1041
00:39:04,160 --> 00:39:08,480
likelihood estimation does. You keep

1042
00:39:06,560 --> 00:39:10,560
trying different underlying images,

1043
00:39:08,480 --> 00:39:13,520
generate simulated measurements, and

1044
00:39:10,560 --> 00:39:15,839
compare them to the actual data.

1045
00:39:13,520 --> 00:39:17,680
When the simulated measurements maximize

1046
00:39:15,839 --> 00:39:19,760
the probability of matching the real

1047
00:39:17,680 --> 00:39:21,760
ones, that candidate is taken as the

1048
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:23,520
true image.

1049
00:39:21,760 --> 00:39:25,760
This approach is fundamentally different

1050
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:28,240
from deterministic CT reconstruction

1051
00:39:25,760 --> 00:39:30,400
methods. Instead of assuming the data is

1052
00:39:28,240 --> 00:39:32,240
exact, it operates within a statistical

1053
00:39:30,400 --> 00:39:33,760
framework.

1054
00:39:32,240 --> 00:39:36,160
In this framework, measurements are

1055
00:39:33,760 --> 00:39:37,440
modeled with a pluson distribution.

1056
00:39:36,160 --> 00:39:39,599
Because of the independence of

1057
00:39:37,440 --> 00:39:41,440
measurements, the overall likelihood can

1058
00:39:39,599 --> 00:39:43,599
be expressed as a product of individual

1059
00:39:41,440 --> 00:39:46,000
probabilities.

1060
00:39:43,599 --> 00:39:48,400
By taking the natural logarithm, this

1061
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:50,400
product is converted into a summation

1062
00:39:48,400 --> 00:39:53,040
making the formulation simpler and more

1063
00:39:50,400 --> 00:39:54,960
practical to compute. The key point is

1064
00:39:53,040 --> 00:39:57,280
that the statistical model accounts for

1065
00:39:54,960 --> 00:40:00,800
the pluson noise inherent in the data

1066
00:39:57,280 --> 00:40:03,040
which deterministic methods ignore.

1067
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:05,680
To see the advantage, consider a phantom

1068
00:40:03,040 --> 00:40:07,760
experiment. If you introduce radioactive

1069
00:40:05,680 --> 00:40:09,920
tracers into the phantom and reconstruct

1070
00:40:07,760 --> 00:40:12,320
with filtered back projection, you are

1071
00:40:09,920 --> 00:40:15,440
assuming no noise that every measurement

1072
00:40:12,320 --> 00:40:17,440
is an exact line integral.

1073
00:40:15,440 --> 00:40:20,000
But in reality, measurements always have

1074
00:40:17,440 --> 00:40:22,160
statistical fluctuations. This mismatch

1075
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:25,280
leads to streaking artifacts and random

1076
00:40:22,160 --> 00:40:27,280
noise in the reconstructed images.

1077
00:40:25,280 --> 00:40:29,520
By contrast, maximum likelihood

1078
00:40:27,280 --> 00:40:31,520
reconstruction explains the fluctuations

1079
00:40:29,520 --> 00:40:34,000
more accurately within the statistical

1080
00:40:31,520 --> 00:40:36,240
model. As a result, the reconstructed

1081
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:38,320
images show fewer artifacts and improve

1082
00:40:36,240 --> 00:40:41,320
quality as demonstrated in the phantom

1083
00:40:38,320 --> 00:40:41,320
example.

1084
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:46,560
This example shows patient

1085
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:48,400
reconstruction results. When using

1086
00:40:46,560 --> 00:40:50,480
filtered back projection, the images

1087
00:40:48,400 --> 00:40:52,960
clearly display streak artifacts and

1088
00:40:50,480 --> 00:40:55,440
significant noise. These issues appear

1089
00:40:52,960 --> 00:40:57,839
across all views, coronal, transaxial,

1090
00:40:55,440 --> 00:40:59,920
and sagittal. Overall, the images

1091
00:40:57,839 --> 00:41:02,160
produced by filtered back projection are

1092
00:40:59,920 --> 00:41:05,839
noisy and contain strong artifacts that

1093
00:41:02,160 --> 00:41:07,680
reduce clarity and diagnostic value.

1094
00:41:05,839 --> 00:41:09,280
In contrast, maximum likelihood

1095
00:41:07,680 --> 00:41:11,119
iterative reconstruction directly

1096
00:41:09,280 --> 00:41:13,280
incorporates statistical knowledge into

1097
00:41:11,119 --> 00:41:15,200
the reconstruction process. This

1098
00:41:13,280 --> 00:41:18,720
approach produces images that are both

1099
00:41:15,200 --> 00:41:20,800
visually and quantitatively improved.

1100
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:22,720
Noise is greatly reduced, artifacts are

1101
00:41:20,800 --> 00:41:24,720
minimized, and the resulting images are

1102
00:41:22,720 --> 00:41:26,400
more accurate and reliable for clinical

1103
00:41:24,720 --> 00:41:28,400
use.

1104
00:41:26,400 --> 00:41:30,400
The side-by-side comparison highlights

1105
00:41:28,400 --> 00:41:32,160
the advantage of maximum likelihood

1106
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:34,880
iterative reconstruction over

1107
00:41:32,160 --> 00:41:36,640
traditional filtered back projection. In

1108
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:39,040
real patient data, the iterative

1109
00:41:36,640 --> 00:41:40,880
approach delivers superior image quality

1110
00:41:39,040 --> 00:41:43,880
both in appearance and in diagnostic

1111
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:43,880
accuracy.

1112
00:41:45,599 --> 00:41:49,599
Let's talk again about PET CT, which

1113
00:41:47,440 --> 00:41:51,760
we've mentioned several times. First,

1114
00:41:49,599 --> 00:41:53,920
the CT image provides structural and

1115
