1231
00:46:21,280 --> 00:46:25,920
By rotating the detector and columators

1232
00:46:23,520 --> 00:46:27,680
around the patient, we get signals from

1233
00:46:25,920 --> 00:46:30,000
different angles, enabling a full

1234
00:46:27,680 --> 00:46:31,839
three-dimensional reconstruction. The

1235
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:34,000
system also allows for standard

1236
00:46:31,839 --> 00:46:39,000
longitudinal scanning, which is what you

1237
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:39,000
typically see in practical clinical use.

1238
00:46:40,480 --> 00:46:45,760
Now building on the idea used in PET CT,

1239
00:46:42,880 --> 00:46:47,760
we can talk about spec systems. In this

1240
00:46:45,760 --> 00:46:49,839
setup, there can be two or even three

1241
00:46:47,760 --> 00:46:52,160
spec cameras working at the same time to

1242
00:46:49,839 --> 00:46:54,319
gather more data.

1243
00:46:52,160 --> 00:46:56,720
If you use only one planer gamma

1244
00:46:54,319 --> 00:46:58,960
detector, a lot of photons, especially

1245
00:46:56,720 --> 00:47:00,880
those coming horizontally and downward,

1246
00:46:58,960 --> 00:47:02,960
will be wasted and their information

1247
00:47:00,880 --> 00:47:04,720
won't be captured.

1248
00:47:02,960 --> 00:47:07,359
But with triple detectors, you can use

1249
00:47:04,720 --> 00:47:09,599
gamma photons much more efficiently. The

1250
00:47:07,359 --> 00:47:11,839
CT part of the system gives you precise

1251
00:47:09,599 --> 00:47:13,920
anatomical information and helps provide

1252
00:47:11,839 --> 00:47:16,000
accurate attenuation correction for the

1253
00:47:13,920 --> 00:47:18,000
spec data.

1254
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:19,920
This means better quantification,

1255
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:22,319
improved image quality, and superior

1256
00:47:19,920 --> 00:47:26,839
diagnostic capability through combined

1257
00:47:22,319 --> 00:47:26,839
anatomical and functional information.

1258
00:47:28,480 --> 00:47:32,319
In pilot imaging, you work with a

1259
00:47:30,079 --> 00:47:34,480
detector ring. This setup can be used

1260
00:47:32,319 --> 00:47:36,960
for both human studies and small animal

1261
00:47:34,480 --> 00:47:38,880
imaging. Small animals are especially

1262
00:47:36,960 --> 00:47:40,800
important in pharmaceutical research

1263
00:47:38,880 --> 00:47:43,520
since drug companies rely on them to

1264
00:47:40,800 --> 00:47:45,200
test potential new drugs.

1265
00:47:43,520 --> 00:47:47,040
The development of a drug typically

1266
00:47:45,200 --> 00:47:48,960
passes through several phases starting

1267
00:47:47,040 --> 00:47:51,119
with cell cultures, then moving to small

1268
00:47:48,960 --> 00:47:53,280
animal studies, followed by pre-clinical

1269
00:47:51,119 --> 00:47:55,760
trials, clinical trials, and eventually

1270
00:47:53,280 --> 00:47:57,839
market approval. Out of hundreds of drug

1271
00:47:55,760 --> 00:47:59,920
candidates tested, only a very small

1272
00:47:57,839 --> 00:48:02,160
fraction ever reach the market. This

1273
00:47:59,920 --> 00:48:05,200
long, costly process is one reason why

1274
00:48:02,160 --> 00:48:07,359
drug development is so expensive.

1275
00:48:05,200 --> 00:48:09,599
The pilot imaging ring itself consists

1276
00:48:07,359 --> 00:48:12,079
of gamma detectors and the associated

1277
00:48:09,599 --> 00:48:14,240
electronic circuitry. These detectors

1278
00:48:12,079 --> 00:48:16,640
not only capture signals but also record

1279
00:48:14,240 --> 00:48:18,880
timing information.

1280
00:48:16,640 --> 00:48:21,359
When multiple gamma photons are detected

1281
00:48:18,880 --> 00:48:23,119
within the same time window, the system

1282
00:48:21,359 --> 00:48:25,520
identifies them as coming from the same

1283
00:48:23,119 --> 00:48:28,000
event and reports a coincidence along a

1284
00:48:25,520 --> 00:48:29,680
line of response.

1285
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:31,440
In this way, the line of response

1286
00:48:29,680 --> 00:48:33,440
corresponds to the radio tracer

1287
00:48:31,440 --> 00:48:35,520
distribution along that line, which is

1288
00:48:33,440 --> 00:48:37,359
essentially a line integral. The

1289
00:48:35,520 --> 00:48:39,280
attenuation coefficient in this

1290
00:48:37,359 --> 00:48:43,319
formulation is treated as independent

1291
00:48:39,280 --> 00:48:43,319
and can be factored out.

1292
00:48:44,720 --> 00:48:48,880
This is a big picture overview. If you

1293
00:48:47,040 --> 00:48:50,880
look at the components in detail,

1294
00:48:48,880 --> 00:48:53,200
multiple figures highlight the power and

1295
00:48:50,880 --> 00:48:54,800
structure of each unit. You start with a

1296
00:48:53,200 --> 00:48:56,720
scintillation crystal at the front of

1297
00:48:54,800 --> 00:48:58,640
each module.

1298
00:48:56,720 --> 00:49:00,640
The cintilation crystal's job is to

1299
00:48:58,640 --> 00:49:02,640
convert incoming gamma photons into

1300
00:49:00,640 --> 00:49:04,640
visible light.

1301
00:49:02,640 --> 00:49:07,359
This visible light is then captured by a

1302
00:49:04,640 --> 00:49:09,680
photo multiplier tube or PMT. The

1303
00:49:07,359 --> 00:49:11,680
crystal and PMT work together. When the

1304
00:49:09,680 --> 00:49:13,599
scintillation crystal creates visible

1305
00:49:11,680 --> 00:49:15,599
light, the photo multiplier tube

1306
00:49:13,599 --> 00:49:17,839
multiplies and amplifies that light

1307
00:49:15,599 --> 00:49:19,599
signal, converting it into an electrical

1308
00:49:17,839 --> 00:49:21,359
current.

1309
00:49:19,599 --> 00:49:23,520
After the signal passes through the

1310
00:49:21,359 --> 00:49:26,880
tube, it goes on to electronic circuits

1311
00:49:23,520 --> 00:49:28,480
that process the electrical impulse.

1312
00:49:26,880 --> 00:49:30,640
Depending on the amplitude of that

1313
00:49:28,480 --> 00:49:33,680
impulse, you can determine the energy of

1314
00:49:30,640 --> 00:49:35,680
the interacting gamma photon.

1315
00:49:33,680 --> 00:49:38,000
One unit like this consisting of an

1316
00:49:35,680 --> 00:49:40,240
array of crystals and a set of PMTs is

1317
00:49:38,000 --> 00:49:42,559
called a block. Several blocks combined

1318
00:49:40,240 --> 00:49:44,720
form a bucket which can include hundreds

1319
00:49:42,559 --> 00:49:46,640
of crystals.

1320
00:49:44,720 --> 00:49:49,040
Multiple buckets are arranged together

1321
00:49:46,640 --> 00:49:51,680
to create the full detector ring which

1322
00:49:49,040 --> 00:49:53,280
collects all the imaging data at once.

1323
00:49:51,680 --> 00:49:54,960
There are additional engineering details

1324
00:49:53,280 --> 00:49:56,720
in how these elements are assembled and

1325
00:49:54,960 --> 00:49:58,559
how the signals are managed. But at its

1326
00:49:56,720 --> 00:50:00,480
core, this system is designed to

1327
00:49:58,559 --> 00:50:01,839
efficiently detect and process gamma

1328
00:50:00,480 --> 00:50:04,839
events for reliable image

1329
00:50:01,839 --> 00:50:04,839
reconstruction.

1330
00:50:06,720 --> 00:50:10,160
Now, let's talk about the desirable

1331
00:50:08,319 --> 00:50:12,240
properties of crystal materials used in

1332
00:50:10,160 --> 00:50:14,319
PET scanners. I'll go through these

1333
00:50:12,240 --> 00:50:16,720
quickly. First, the crystal should have

1334
00:50:14,319 --> 00:50:18,640
a high density, which gives it a large

1335
00:50:16,720 --> 00:50:20,960
effective cross-section for interacting

1336
00:50:18,640 --> 00:50:23,280
with gamma rays.

1337
00:50:20,960 --> 00:50:25,200
Next, you want a large effective atomic

1338
00:50:23,280 --> 00:50:27,119
number. This increases gammaray

1339
00:50:25,200 --> 00:50:29,920
detection efficiency by boosting

1340
00:50:27,119 --> 00:50:31,520
photoelectric absorption. The decay time

1341
00:50:29,920 --> 00:50:33,920
should be short. You need the crystal to

1342
00:50:31,520 --> 00:50:36,079
release its light energy very quickly.

1343
00:50:33,920 --> 00:50:38,319
If the decay time is too long, then each

1344
00:50:36,079 --> 00:50:40,640
photon event can overlap in time,

1345
00:50:38,319 --> 00:50:43,119
causing loss in timing resolution and

1346
00:50:40,640 --> 00:50:45,680
poor image quality.

1347
00:50:43,119 --> 00:50:47,760
A highlight yield is also important. The

1348
00:50:45,680 --> 00:50:50,319
crystal must convert as much gamma

1349
00:50:47,760 --> 00:50:52,400
energy as possible into visible light,

1350
00:50:50,319 --> 00:50:54,480
maximizing signal for efficient

1351
00:50:52,400 --> 00:50:56,480
detection.

1352
00:50:54,480 --> 00:50:59,200
Ideally, the wavelength of the emitted

1353
00:50:56,480 --> 00:51:00,880
light should be near 400 nanome since

1354
00:50:59,200 --> 00:51:02,880
photo multiplier tubes are most

1355
00:51:00,880 --> 00:51:05,359
sensitive in this range, making the

1356
00:51:02,880 --> 00:51:07,280
system more efficient.

1357
00:51:05,359 --> 00:51:10,400
Additionally, the refractive index

1358
00:51:07,280 --> 00:51:12,960
should be close to 1.5 to ensure optimal

1359
00:51:10,400 --> 00:51:15,839
optical coupling between the crystal and

1360
00:51:12,960 --> 00:51:18,319
the photo multiplier tube.

1361
00:51:15,839 --> 00:51:20,000
The crystal must also be non-hyroscopic,

1362
00:51:18,319 --> 00:51:22,000
meaning it should not absorb moisture

1363
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:24,160
from the air.

1364
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:26,240
If it does, it can degrade and turn into

1365
00:51:24,160 --> 00:51:28,720
powder, which would make it unusable for

1366
00:51:26,240 --> 00:51:30,720
detector fabrication. All these

1367
00:51:28,720 --> 00:51:32,880
properties are critical for reliable

1368
00:51:30,720 --> 00:51:35,880
higherformance medical imaging with PET

1369
00:51:32,880 --> 00:51:35,880
scanners.

1370
00:51:37,520 --> 00:51:41,680
All of these are considered desirable

1371
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:43,440
properties for PET detector crystals.

1372
00:51:41,680 --> 00:51:46,400
The table lists the main candidate

1373
00:51:43,440 --> 00:51:48,000
materials used in PET scanners.

1374
00:51:46,400 --> 00:51:50,800
You don't need to memorize all the

1375
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:52,559
details or specific values here. In

1376
00:51:50,800 --> 00:51:54,559
clinical practice, it's more important

1377
00:51:52,559 --> 00:51:56,640
to understand the general differences

1378
00:51:54,559 --> 00:51:59,280
and why a particular material might be

1379
00:51:56,640 --> 00:52:00,960
chosen for a given system.

1380
00:51:59,280 --> 00:52:03,359
What matters is knowing that these

1381
00:52:00,960 --> 00:52:05,920
materials differ in their density, decay

1382
00:52:03,359 --> 00:52:08,240
time, emission intensity, atomic number,

1383
00:52:05,920 --> 00:52:11,040
refractive index, and whether they are

1384
00:52:08,240 --> 00:52:12,720
hyroscopic. Each property affects how

1385
00:52:11,040 --> 00:52:15,119
well the crystal works for detecting

1386
00:52:12,720 --> 00:52:18,119
gamma rays and producing highquality PET

1387
00:52:15,119 --> 00:52:18,119
images.

1388
00:52:19,680 --> 00:52:23,359
There aren't many engineering details

1389
00:52:21,520 --> 00:52:25,920
here, but this is mainly for those

1390
00:52:23,359 --> 00:52:27,839
working in detector development. The key

1391
00:52:25,920 --> 00:52:29,440
components for PET detectors start with

1392
00:52:27,839 --> 00:52:32,000
the cintilation crystals, which are

1393
00:52:29,440 --> 00:52:33,920
organized into blocks.

1394
00:52:32,000 --> 00:52:35,599
Each block is coupled with multiple

1395
00:52:33,920 --> 00:52:38,000
photo multiplier tubes in the

1396
00:52:35,599 --> 00:52:40,160
background, typically four per block.

1397
00:52:38,000 --> 00:52:41,920
These blocks are then assembled into a

1398
00:52:40,160 --> 00:52:43,839
complete detector ring that surrounds

1399
00:52:41,920 --> 00:52:46,079
the patient.

1400
00:52:43,839 --> 00:52:48,640
As the PET system operates, the crystals

1401
00:52:46,079 --> 00:52:50,960
detect incoming gamma photons and the

1402
00:52:48,640 --> 00:52:52,800
photo multiplier tubes convert the light

1403
00:52:50,960 --> 00:52:55,119
produced by these interactions into

1404
00:52:52,800 --> 00:52:57,520
electrical signals.

1405
00:52:55,119 --> 00:52:59,520
The systems processing unit precisely

1406
00:52:57,520 --> 00:53:01,760
monitors the electrical signals to

1407
00:52:59,520 --> 00:53:04,800
determine exactly when and where events

1408
00:53:01,760 --> 00:53:06,880
have occurred and at what energy. The

1409
00:53:04,800 --> 00:53:10,800
main gamma photon from a PET scan

1410
00:53:06,880 --> 00:53:12,880
carries 511 keV energy.

1411
00:53:10,800 --> 00:53:15,359
But if there is multiple scattering such

1412
00:53:12,880 --> 00:53:17,520
as Compton scattering inside the body,

1413
00:53:15,359 --> 00:53:19,119
energy is lost and can complicate the

1414
00:53:17,520 --> 00:53:21,040
signal.

1415
00:53:19,119 --> 00:53:23,440
The electronics and coincidence

1416
00:53:21,040 --> 00:53:26,079
processing unit integrate all detected

1417
00:53:23,440 --> 00:53:28,400
events and finally this data is sent to

1418
00:53:26,079 --> 00:53:30,319
the computer system.

1419
00:53:28,400 --> 00:53:32,319
After data acquisition, image

1420
00:53:30,319 --> 00:53:34,640
reconstruction begins, often with

1421
00:53:32,319 --> 00:53:38,160
methods such as filtered back projection

1422
00:53:34,640 --> 00:53:40,079
to produce cross-sectional images.

1423
00:53:38,160 --> 00:53:42,400
All of these steps come together to make

1424
00:53:40,079 --> 00:53:44,800
PET imaging possible. From crystal

1425
00:53:42,400 --> 00:53:47,040
blocks and photo multiplier tubes to

1426
00:53:44,800 --> 00:53:50,040
data integration and final image

1427
00:53:47,040 --> 00:53:50,040
creation,

1428
00:53:51,760 --> 00:53:56,240
the technical advancement in PAT imaging

1429
00:53:54,160 --> 00:53:59,119
has been driven largely by improvements

1430
00:53:56,240 --> 00:54:01,119
in detector technology.

1431
00:53:59,119 --> 00:54:04,319
Over the years, we've seen a progression

1432
00:54:01,119 --> 00:54:06,880
from NAI detectors to BGO and now to

1433
00:54:04,319 --> 00:54:10,160
LSO. With each milestone year bringing

1434
00:54:06,880 --> 00:54:12,240
significant gains in image quality,

1435
00:54:10,160 --> 00:54:13,920
these different generations of detectors

1436
00:54:12,240 --> 00:54:16,880
reflect long-term innovation in

1437
00:54:13,920 --> 00:54:18,640
materials engineering and system design.

1438
00:54:16,880 --> 00:54:20,559
As you can see, each new detector

1439
00:54:18,640 --> 00:54:23,280
technology has resulted in clearer,

1440
00:54:20,559 --> 00:54:25,359
higher resolution images.

1441
00:54:23,280 --> 00:54:27,040
This slide is just for your information.

1442
00:54:25,359 --> 00:54:28,720
And I won't spend too much time on it,

1443
00:54:27,040 --> 00:54:30,800
but it's important to recognize how

1444
00:54:28,720 --> 00:54:32,800
advancements in detector design have

1445
00:54:30,800 --> 00:54:34,400
directly impacted the quality of PET

1446
00:54:32,800 --> 00:54:37,400
images throughout the history of the

1447
00:54:34,400 --> 00:54:37,400
field.

1448
00:54:38,640 --> 00:54:42,800
For a system to work at its best, noise,

1449
00:54:40,960 --> 00:54:45,359
scatter, and random events must be

1450
00:54:42,800 --> 00:54:47,520
estimated and removed. This slide is a

1451
00:54:45,359 --> 00:54:49,200
nice summary. A true event means you

1452
00:54:47,520 --> 00:54:51,040
have a single annihilation. Then two

1453
00:54:49,200 --> 00:54:53,359
photons are emitted and detected by

1454
00:54:51,040 --> 00:54:55,119
opposite detectors and captured by the

1455
00:54:53,359 --> 00:54:57,119
circuit.

1456
00:54:55,119 --> 00:54:59,040
That's what you really want. These true

1457
00:54:57,119 --> 00:55:00,720
coincidences are the signals that

1458
00:54:59,040 --> 00:55:02,960
contribute to accurate image

1459
00:55:00,720 --> 00:55:04,240
reconstruction.

1460
00:55:02,960 --> 00:55:07,040
Scatter events have different

1461
00:55:04,240 --> 00:55:08,880
mechanisms. Here's an example. A photon

1462
00:55:07,040 --> 00:55:10,720
is emitted from an event, but gets

1463
00:55:08,880 --> 00:55:13,040
scattered away before it reaches the

1464
00:55:10,720 --> 00:55:14,960
detector. The system may report a

1465
00:55:13,040 --> 00:55:18,319
coincidence along this scattered path,

1466
00:55:14,960 --> 00:55:20,079
but it's not the true line of response.

1467
00:55:18,319 --> 00:55:21,760
This isn't the real signal we need. So,

1468
00:55:20,079 --> 00:55:23,520
the scattered photon event should be

1469
00:55:21,760 --> 00:55:26,160
removed. Just like in CT where

1470
00:55:23,520 --> 00:55:28,319
scattering also occurs,

1471
00:55:26,160 --> 00:55:30,400
random events are different. In this

1472
00:55:28,319 --> 00:55:32,240
case, two gamma photons are generated

1473
00:55:30,400 --> 00:55:34,400
from different events in the body and

1474
00:55:32,240 --> 00:55:36,640
one photon is detected in the same time

1475
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:38,240
window as another photon from a separate

1476
00:55:36,640 --> 00:55:40,240
event.

1477
00:55:38,240 --> 00:55:42,160
The system reports a coincidence, but

1478
00:55:40,240 --> 00:55:44,480
along a line that doesn't correspond to

1479
00:55:42,160 --> 00:55:47,520
either true event. This is not accurate

1480
00:55:44,480 --> 00:55:49,920
and should be avoided if possible.

1481
00:55:47,520 --> 00:55:52,079
In 3D PET imaging, you can see that

1482
00:55:49,920 --> 00:55:53,920
scattered and random events become more

1483
00:55:52,079 --> 00:55:56,240
frequent, mainly because sensitivity

1484
00:55:53,920 --> 00:55:58,240
increases due to the wider aperture and

1485
00:55:56,240 --> 00:56:00,240
more possibilities for 3D data

1486
00:55:58,240 --> 00:56:02,000
acquisition.

1487
00:56:00,240 --> 00:56:04,400
So, it's important to maximize

1488
00:56:02,000 --> 00:56:09,240
sensitivity while minimizing scatter and

1489
00:56:04,400 --> 00:56:09,240
random events to maintain image quality.

1490
00:56:10,720 --> 00:56:14,960
How can you estimate the scattered

1491
00:56:12,400 --> 00:56:16,559
background? This is actually simple. You

1492
00:56:14,960 --> 00:56:18,880
set the energy window around the main

1493
00:56:16,559 --> 00:56:20,559
photo peak. That's the main window right

1494
00:56:18,880 --> 00:56:22,079
at the peak and you collect counts

1495
00:56:20,559 --> 00:56:23,920
there.

1496
00:56:22,079 --> 00:56:25,680
Then you also set windows on the left

1497
00:56:23,920 --> 00:56:28,319
and right. These are the low and high

1498
00:56:25,680 --> 00:56:30,480
energy side windows. The idea is that

1499
00:56:28,319 --> 00:56:33,760
you know the primary gamma photon should

1500
00:56:30,480 --> 00:56:35,760
be at 511 keV. So its energy falls in

1501
00:56:33,760 --> 00:56:37,680
the main window.

1502
00:56:35,760 --> 00:56:40,960
If there are scattered photons, their

1503
00:56:37,680 --> 00:56:42,559
energy will be lower than 511 keV. So

1504
00:56:40,960 --> 00:56:44,160
you'll see them in the lower energy

1505
00:56:42,559 --> 00:56:46,079
window.

1506
00:56:44,160 --> 00:56:47,760
If there are random events or noise,

1507
00:56:46,079 --> 00:56:50,319
these could appear in the higher energy

1508
00:56:47,760 --> 00:56:52,480
window by collecting data in both the

1509
00:56:50,319 --> 00:56:54,079
lower and higher side windows. You have

1510
00:56:52,480 --> 00:56:55,920
a way to estimate the background

1511
00:56:54,079 --> 00:56:58,079
scattering.

1512
00:56:55,920 --> 00:57:00,240
You then assign weights to the counts in

1513
00:56:58,079 --> 00:57:01,920
the lower and higher windows. This

1514
00:57:00,240 --> 00:57:03,760
weighted combination gives you an

1515
00:57:01,920 --> 00:57:05,760
estimate of the scattered background in

1516
00:57:03,760 --> 00:57:07,680
the main window.

1517
00:57:05,760 --> 00:57:09,440
With that estimate, you subtract the

1518
00:57:07,680 --> 00:57:12,079
scattered contribution from the main

1519
00:57:09,440 --> 00:57:14,160
window, giving you the corrected signal.

1520
00:57:12,079 --> 00:57:16,160
This process lets you measure and remove

1521
00:57:14,160 --> 00:57:20,760
the scatter components. So your final

1522
00:57:16,160 --> 00:57:20,760
image is much cleaner and more accurate.

1523
00:57:22,319 --> 00:57:26,480
So this shows you the result. This is

1524
00:57:24,640 --> 00:57:28,160
the real measurement and you can see the

1525
00:57:26,480 --> 00:57:30,720
scattered background as it appears in

1526
00:57:28,160 --> 00:57:32,319
the data. When you remove the scattered

1527
00:57:30,720 --> 00:57:33,520
background, you get these corrected

1528
00:57:32,319 --> 00:57:35,920
measurements or corrected

1529
00:57:33,520 --> 00:57:37,839
reconstructions.

1530
00:57:35,920 --> 00:57:40,079
This demonstrates how the result changes

1531
00:57:37,839 --> 00:57:42,000
by addressing the scattering problem.

1532
00:57:40,079 --> 00:57:44,240
Scatter correction is a key point in

1533
00:57:42,000 --> 00:57:47,240
improving measurement accuracy and image

1534
00:57:44,240 --> 00:57:47,240
quality.

1535
00:57:48,880 --> 00:57:52,880
And for random correction, this is a

1536
00:57:50,640 --> 00:57:54,799
very clever idea. If you have random

1537
00:57:52,880 --> 00:57:57,440
events being recorded, they introduce

1538
00:57:54,799 --> 00:57:59,520
noise into your data. For usual data

1539
00:57:57,440 --> 00:58:01,760
processing, these random events really

1540
00:57:59,520 --> 00:58:03,520
complicate things.

1541
00:58:01,760 --> 00:58:05,280
But we can address this purposely. For

1542
00:58:03,520 --> 00:58:07,200
each part of the detector, we introduce

1543
00:58:05,280 --> 00:58:09,200
a delay.

1544
00:58:07,200 --> 00:58:10,960
If it's a random coincidence, then the

1545
00:58:09,200 --> 00:58:12,960
probability of the random event with

1546
00:58:10,960 --> 00:58:15,520
this result delay is the same as without

1547
00:58:12,960 --> 00:58:18,240
the delay. This is the key statement.

1548
00:58:15,520 --> 00:58:20,079
This is the important idea.

1549
00:58:18,240 --> 00:58:22,480
By introducing a delay in the

1550
00:58:20,079 --> 00:58:24,799
coincidence detection, the probability

1551
00:58:22,480 --> 00:58:27,119
line for incident and delayed results is

1552
00:58:24,799 --> 00:58:29,599
the same because the random coincidences

1553
00:58:27,119 --> 00:58:31,440
depend on the average gamma ray activity

1554
00:58:29,599 --> 00:58:34,079
which is assumed to be more or less

1555
00:58:31,440 --> 00:58:36,559
constant over time. The delay itself

1556
00:58:34,079 --> 00:58:38,480
doesn't change that rate.

1557
00:58:36,559 --> 00:58:40,319
You can then use the measured random

1558
00:58:38,480 --> 00:58:42,480
events from the delayed window to

1559
00:58:40,319 --> 00:58:44,720
directly estimate and subtract out the

1560
00:58:42,480 --> 00:58:46,400
random event contribution from your true

1561
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:48,000
data.

1562
00:58:46,400 --> 00:58:50,240
This approach gives you higher quality,

1563
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:54,520
more quantitative results. That's the

1564
00:58:50,240 --> 00:58:54,520
whole idea behind random correction.

1565
00:58:56,079 --> 00:58:59,839
There are multiple high order effects in

1566
00:58:58,240 --> 00:59:02,319
PET imaging and this is what I'm showing

1567
00:58:59,839 --> 00:59:04,160
here with these figures. Ideally, you

1568
00:59:02,319 --> 00:59:06,240
want to detect coincidences. By

1569
00:59:04,160 --> 00:59:08,319
definition, in a given time window, you

1570
00:59:06,240 --> 00:59:10,160
should only have two gamma ray photons

1571
00:59:08,319 --> 00:59:12,319
captured.

1572
00:59:10,160 --> 00:59:14,000
If your time window is short enough, you

1573
00:59:12,319 --> 00:59:16,160
get a well-controlled count rate,

1574
00:59:14,000 --> 00:59:17,359
typically up to 100,000 per second,

1575
00:59:16,160 --> 00:59:19,599
which is the principle behind

1576
00:59:17,359 --> 00:59:21,760
coincidence detection. However,

1577
00:59:19,599 --> 00:59:23,920
sometimes within a short time interval,

1578
00:59:21,760 --> 00:59:25,920
you might have three gammaray photons

1579
00:59:23,920 --> 00:59:29,119
reported simultaneously.

1580
00:59:25,920 --> 00:59:30,960
How does this happen?

1581
00:59:29,119 --> 00:59:33,200
For example, the first annihilation

1582
00:59:30,960 --> 00:59:35,280
event is detected near detector 2. So,

1583
00:59:33,200 --> 00:59:37,599
its gamma photon reaches that detector

1584
00:59:35,280 --> 00:59:39,760
and is recorded.

1585
00:59:37,599 --> 00:59:42,319
Within that same time window, another

1586
00:59:39,760 --> 00:59:45,200
event occurs closer to detector 13 and

1587
00:59:42,319 --> 00:59:47,119
its sister photon travels a longer path.

1588
00:59:45,200 --> 00:59:49,200
Before the photon from the second event

1589
00:59:47,119 --> 00:59:50,720
reaches detector 5, the third photon

1590
00:59:49,200 --> 00:59:52,880
from the first event might reach

1591
00:59:50,720 --> 00:59:54,640
detector 10.

1592
00:59:52,880 --> 00:59:58,319
So, within the same interval, three

1593
00:59:54,640 --> 01:00:00,480
photons can be detected as coincidences.

1594
00:59:58,319 --> 01:00:02,400
Whenever you get triple coincidences,

1595
01:00:00,480 --> 01:00:04,240
three detected photons in the same

1596
01:00:02,400 --> 01:00:06,319
resolving window, they introduce

1597
01:00:04,240 --> 01:00:08,000
confusion and you simply discard that

1598
01:00:06,319 --> 01:00:09,839
data.

1599
01:00:08,000 --> 01:00:12,480
The system is designed to expect only

1600
01:00:09,839 --> 01:00:14,160
pairs of coincident photons. If you see

1601
01:00:12,480 --> 01:00:17,760
this higher order event, you just throw

1602
01:00:14,160 --> 01:00:20,160
it away, focusing only on true doubles.

1603
01:00:17,760 --> 01:00:21,760
But confusing situations remain. It's

1604
01:00:20,160 --> 01:00:24,319
hard to know which event should be

1605
01:00:21,760 --> 01:00:25,760
assigned to which lines of response.

1606
01:00:24,319 --> 01:00:28,720
That's where the correction factor

1607
01:00:25,760 --> 01:00:30,400
formula comes in. For every true

1608
01:00:28,720 --> 01:00:32,079
coincidence measurement, you also need

1609
01:00:30,400 --> 01:00:34,799
to account for the data loss due to

1610
01:00:32,079 --> 01:00:36,559
triple or higher order coincidences. The

1611
01:00:34,799 --> 01:00:38,799
formula shown here calculates the

1612
01:00:36,559 --> 01:00:40,960
correction factor, combining both the

1613
01:00:38,799 --> 01:00:43,200
estimated loss from discarded high order

1614
01:00:40,960 --> 01:00:45,280
events and the true measurement to help

1615
01:00:43,200 --> 01:00:46,880
restore your measured data to the real

1616
01:00:45,280 --> 01:00:48,880
level.

1617
01:00:46,880 --> 01:00:50,880
Sometimes higher order events may be

1618
01:00:48,880 --> 01:00:52,640
very rare, but if your time window is

1619
01:00:50,880 --> 01:00:54,880
wide enough, you might encounter even

1620
01:00:52,640 --> 01:00:57,440
more photons simultaneously, which

1621
01:00:54,880 --> 01:00:59,440
complicates things further.

1622
01:00:57,440 --> 01:01:01,520
In practice, these cases are always

1623
01:00:59,440 --> 01:01:04,079
discarded, and the formula allows you to

1624
01:01:01,520 --> 01:01:06,240
estimate the amount of lost data. You

1625
01:01:04,079 --> 01:01:08,079
then use this correction factor to scale

1626
01:01:06,240 --> 01:01:10,559
your results back to the true count,

1627
01:01:08,079 --> 01:01:13,559
improving accuracy in quantitative PET

1628
01:01:10,559 --> 01:01:13,559
measurements.

1629
01:01:15,280 --> 01:01:20,000
So graphically you can see this process

1630
01:01:17,440 --> 01:01:21,920
clearly. The total counts the overall

1631
01:01:20,000 --> 01:01:24,559
measured signal or image actually

1632
01:01:21,920 --> 01:01:26,559
contain two main components. These two

1633
01:01:24,559 --> 01:01:28,559
components are scatter background and

1634
01:01:26,559 --> 01:01:31,200
random counts along with a variety of

1635
01:01:28,559 --> 01:01:33,200
other effects and oddities.

1636
01:01:31,200 --> 01:01:34,960
You can estimate the random count by

1637
01:01:33,200 --> 01:01:37,520
using a delayed window and triple

1638
01:01:34,960 --> 01:01:39,440
coincidences can be corrected as well.

1639
01:01:37,520 --> 01:01:41,119
There are many correction methods, but

1640
01:01:39,440 --> 01:01:42,880
the most important thing to understand

1641
01:01:41,119 --> 01:01:46,160
from this lecture is to have a rough

1642
01:01:42,880 --> 01:01:47,680
idea of how these corrections work.

1643
01:01:46,160 --> 01:01:49,599
All these corrections, including

1644
01:01:47,680 --> 01:01:51,440
attenuation correction, scatter

1645
01:01:49,599 --> 01:01:53,920
correction, random correction, and

1646
01:01:51,440 --> 01:01:56,079
corrections for dead time are essential

1647
01:01:53,920 --> 01:01:57,760
to refine the measured signal and make

1648
01:01:56,079 --> 01:02:02,440
sure your results reflect the true

1649
01:01:57,760 --> 01:02:02,440
activity as accurately as possible.

1650
01:02:03,920 --> 01:02:08,079
The goal is to make your line integral

1651
01:02:06,000 --> 01:02:10,559
data accurately reflect the true line

1652
01:02:08,079 --> 01:02:12,559
integrals through the body. This allows

1653
01:02:10,559 --> 01:02:14,559
you to reconstruct images that truly

1654
01:02:12,559 --> 01:02:17,440
represent the concentration of radio

1655
01:02:14,559 --> 01:02:19,200
tracer in the organ of interest.

1656
01:02:17,440 --> 01:02:20,960
You can do this for each organ and at

1657
01:02:19,200 --> 01:02:23,280
each time point during the PET imaging

1658
01:02:20,960 --> 01:02:24,960
session. By plotting these time curves,

1659
01:02:23,280 --> 01:02:26,960
you can assess profusion in different

1660
01:02:24,960 --> 01:02:29,359
regions and extract physiologically

1661
01:02:26,960 --> 01:02:31,520
relevant parameters.

1662
01:02:29,359 --> 01:02:33,280
This process gives you more diagnostic

1663
01:02:31,520 --> 01:02:35,359
